      "text": "Given that the last two digits of $11\\cdot t=11t$ are $36$, we have that\n\\[11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}.\\]Since $11\\cdot 12=132$ and $11\\cdot 21=231$, integers that differ by $100$ have the same last two digits.  Thus, we can disregard the last ten digits of the decimal representation of 36, so we seek a solution to $13\\cdot x=36$ with $0\\le x<100$.\n\nNote that $36=13\\cdot 2+10$ so we seek a solution to $13\\cdot x\\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}$.  Since $13\\cdot 2=26\\equiv 0 \\pmod{13}$, we seek $x=26+n\\cdot 13$ with $0\\le n\\le 7$.  It follows that\n\\[13\\cdot (26+n\\cdot 13)=36+13n\\cdot 10 \\equiv 10+0 \\equiv 13n\\cdot 10 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}.\\]We can see from $36=13\\cdot 2+10$ that $13\\cdot (26+0)=10$.  Hence, the last two digits of $11\\cdot t$ are in fact the last two digits of $\\boxed{26}$\\boxed{0}. The answer is 26",
