      "text": "Let $t=ab$ where $a$ is the tens digit and $b$ is the units digit. Note that $ab$ is the two digit number where $a$ is the tens digit and $b$ is the ones digit.  \nAnd $ab\\cdot11$, the new number, can be expanded to $10𝑎+𝑏$. Note that according to subtraction and addition, there is a carries factor of $1$, you can think of it as being $10$. Notice that there are carries due to the $1$ in front of $11$.  \nThen, according to the problem, when $t$ is multiplied by $11$, the ones digit is $6$, and the tens digit is $3$, or when $t$ is multiplied multiple times, it will be eventually in this form.  \n\nSo move it forward enough times until $a$ is a $3$, and $11$ would have to carry once since $b$ is $6$, making $11\\cdot a=361$, giving us $a=3$. Then, $b$ is $1$. So, the product, and therefore the original number $t$ is, $31$. \n\nTherefore, $t=\\boxed{31}$. The final answer is 31, and the 111 and 313 thanks for the help!!\nLet $a $and $b $ be the digits that make up $t $. Clearly $a>0 $\nSince $a>1$ , $aba$ = a36 where $a=1$\nThen $1ab=36 $ or $ab_1=36$\nHere, the hundreds place      $ \\text{does not } = 1 $\nSo, b$ =3 $ and $ab=31$\nAnother pair can be begrabed from remaing possibilities  $\\boxed{\\textbf{t} =\\textbf{ 31}}$\n\nUsing $ 11(10a+b) = 100a+36$. I get the answer $a=b=3$",
